[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me take a second to structure this.

So revenue is up 15% but net margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, roughly three-quarters of the margin gone. Since revenue grew, this is almost certainly a cost problem, not a demand problem.

I'd break it into three buckets. First, revenue quality: is the 15% growth coming from more transactions, higher ticket, or new store openings? Growth from new stores can drag margin if those stores are immature. Second, COGS — coffee beans, dairy, cups, syrups — on a per-unit basis. Third, operating costs: labor, rent, utilities, and any corporate overhead.

I'd start with COGS and labor since those are usually the two biggest lines in a coffee chain and the most likely to have moved on a two-year horizon.

To start: do we have a cost breakdown as a percentage of revenue for both the start and end of that two-year period?
