[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:35] Hold that request for a moment; we'll come back to the cost breakdown. Looking at your three buckets, is that structure MECE, and what, if anything, is missing?

[candidate · 3:05] Fair challenge. It's mostly MECE but not fully. Revenue quality isn't really a cost bucket — it's a driver that sits above the other two, so I'm mixing levels. Cleaner version: Profit = Revenue minus Costs. Under revenue, volume (transactions, store count) and price/mix. Under costs, COGS, labor, occupancy, and corporate overhead, each split into price-per-unit and quantity consumed.

What's missing: anything below the operating line. Net margin, not gross or operating, so interest expense on new debt, D&A from a build-out, taxes, or one-time charges could be doing real work here. If they opened stores fast, depreciation and interest would both jump without touching unit economics at all.

Also missing: waste and shrink, which in coffee is a real line and hides inside COGS.

So I'd tighten it to revenue drivers, then cost drivers split by price versus quantity, then below-the-line items.
