[candidate · 4:39] COGS, clearly. Let me tie it out: margin fell 18 points. COGS up 16 points, overhead up 2, labor flat — that's 18. So COGS explains roughly 89% of the decline. Everything else is noise.

Now, 58% COGS on a coffee chain is extreme. Specialty coffee traditionally runs 25–35% on beverage COGS because the whole model is cheap inputs and high markup. So something structural changed.

I'd split COGS two ways. First by input: coffee beans, dairy and alternative milks, syrups and flavorings, packaging, food items. Second by cause: did unit input prices rise, did we consume more per drink, or did the product mix shift toward more expensive drinks — think oat milk lattes and elaborate cold brews versus a drip coffee.

Those point to very different fixes, so I need to separate them.

Do we have COGS broken out by input category, and ideally how each moved over the two years?
