[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 4:45] Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years.

[candidate · 6:15] Let me check whether input inflation alone explains it.

Two years ago COGS was 42 points of revenue. Beans were 25% of that — 10.5 points. Everything else was 75% — 31.5 points.

Apply the inflation: beans 10.5 × 1.40 = 14.7. Other inputs 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3. Total = 58.0 points.

That's exactly where COGS sits today. So input price inflation alone fully accounts for the move. There's no evidence of waste, portion creep, or mix shift toward costlier drinks — and importantly, no evidence of any pass-through, because if menu prices had risen, COGS as a percent of revenue would have come in below 58.

That matters a lot for the recommendation, so let me confirm: do we have menu price history over the two years, and the split of the 15% revenue growth between transaction volume, ticket size, and new stores?
