[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 6:18] Menu prices have not changed in two years. Average revenue per store is $2.4M a year. 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit.

[candidate · 7:48] Let me run the volume math. Revenue per store $2.4M, 200 stores, so $480M today; two years ago $480M/1.15 = $417M, or about $2.087M per store.

Transactions per store today: $2.4M / $6.80 = roughly 353,000. Two years ago: $2.087M / $6.20 = roughly 337,000. So transaction count is up only about 5%; the other 10% of growth is bigger baskets.

Here's what I find important. Menu prices are flat, so every dollar of input inflation hit the P&L unprotected. But also — customers already gave us 10% more revenue per visit, and margin still collapsed to 6%. That tells me the incremental items are not margin-accretive; they're carrying COGS at or above the blended rate. So "get more revenue per customer" has already been run as an experiment and it didn't relieve anything.

That pushes me toward attacking input cost per drink rather than the top line. Do we have cost-per-unit by drink type, or supplier counts?
