[candidate · 9:30] I'll assume no drink-level or supplier data and reason from what we have.

Three lever families.

One, input cost per drink — reformulation. Beans are only 25% of COGS; the other 75% grew almost as fast and is bigger in absolute terms. Portion standardization, default milk choices, syrup dosing, right-sizing cups and lids. A single point of COGS here is $4.8M on $480M.

Two, procurement structure. 200 locations is real scale. Consolidating to fewer suppliers, longer contracts, forward-buying or hedging beans. Coffee and dairy are both commodity-linked and both volatile — flat menu prices meant we took 100% of that volatility.

Three, mix engineering without price changes — steering customers toward drinks we already make profitably through menu layout and defaults, rather than charging more.

I'd deprioritize labor at 22% and flat, and overhead's 2 points is small relative to 16.

Do we know if procurement is centralized or store-by-store?
