[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 9:37] That's not in the information I have. Given everything we've covered, what's your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate · 11:07] My recommendation: do not raise menu prices. Instead, attack COGS directly through recipe reformulation on the highest-cost drinks and a supplier consolidation play.

The reasoning. COGS explains 16 of the 18 points of margin loss, and my math showed input inflation alone accounts for the entire move — 10.5 points of beans at 1.40 plus 31.5 points of other inputs at 1.375 lands exactly on 58. So this is a unit-cost problem, not a revenue problem.

Why I'm rejecting pricing, including selective pricing on premium drinks. Customers already handed us 10% more per ticket — $6.20 to $6.80 — and margin still fell to 6%. The incremental items were not margin-accretive. We effectively ran the "extract more per visit" experiment and it bought us nothing. Selective increases on premium drinks would likely push mix toward cheaper items without fixing the per-drink cost structure. And transaction growth is only 5%, so traffic isn't strong enough to absorb an elasticity hit.

Reformulation and procurement both lower cost per drink without testing demand.

Risks: reformulation can hurt quality perception; consolidation reduces supplier leverage if one fails.

Next steps: pull cost-per-unit by drink, map current supplier count, and pilot reformulation in 10 stores.
