[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:12] You rejected price increases because the bigger baskets didn't relieve margin. Walk me through that logic, given that menu prices were flat while input costs rose.

[candidate · 12:42] Fair push, and let me be precise about what I'm claiming.

You're right that flat prices against rising inputs mechanically guarantees margin compression — that part isn't evidence about pricing power. What the basket data tells me is something narrower: the mix of items customers added was not margin-accretive. If those extra items had carried healthy margins, COGS as a share of revenue would have come in below 58%. It didn't — it landed exactly on the inflation-only number. So the incremental volume diluted rather than helped.

That's a mix statement, not an elasticity statement. I'll concede I can't prove from this data that a price increase would fail.

But my recommendation doesn't rest on elasticity. It rests on sequencing. A price increase restores margin only as long as it outruns inflation, and beans and dairy moved 37–40% in two years — we'd be re-pricing continually. Reformulation and consolidation lower the cost base permanently and make any future pricing move more valuable. I'd fix the denominator first.
