[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:48] You said that if the extra items had carried healthy margins, COGS as a share of revenue would have come in below 58%. Walk me through that. What baseline are you comparing 58% against, and what does it show about those items?

[candidate · 14:18] The baseline is a counterfactual: take the old cost structure and inflate it, holding mix constant. Beans 10.5 points × 1.40 = 14.7, other inputs 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3, total 58.0. That's what COGS would be today if customers bought exactly the same basket as two years ago, just at new input prices.

Actual is 58%. So the mix effect is approximately zero.

What that implies is narrower than "the new items are bad." It implies the incremental items carry roughly the same COGS-to-price ratio as the existing blended basket — about 58 cents of input per dollar. They're not dilutive relative to the average, but they're not accretive either. A genuinely high-margin add-on, a pastry or a drip refill, would have pulled the blended ratio down below 58.

Two caveats I should flag. One, I'm applying those inflation rates to two-year-old weights, so it's approximate. Two, the rates are close enough — 37.5 versus 40 — that the result isn't sensitive to how mix shifted between the categories.

So: volume growth isn't rescuing us. The ratio itself has to change.
