[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 14:20] Understood. You've now said the incremental items were both diluting and neutral to the blend. Which one is your claim, and does your recommendation change depending on the answer?

[candidate · 15:50] Neutral is my claim. I overstated it earlier when I said "diluting" — that was sloppy. The math supports one thing: the mix effect is approximately zero, so the incremental items carry roughly the blended COGS ratio of 58 cents on the dollar. They're margin-neutral relative to the average, not value-destroying.

Does it change the recommendation? Not materially, and here's why. The point I actually need is that 10% more revenue per ticket did nothing to relieve the ratio — and under the neutral reading that's still true, just for a more boring reason. Adding more volume at the blended margin can't fix a blended margin problem. You can't scale your way out of a unit-economics problem when the incremental unit looks like the average unit.

Where it does matter: under the dilutive reading, I'd want to prune or re-engineer the add-on items. Under the neutral reading, I wouldn't — they're fine, they're just not the lever. That makes reformulation and procurement even more clearly the right focus, because there's no easy mix fix hiding in the basket.
